          "id": "py-quiz-dict-keys",
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Which of these **can't** be used as a dict key?",
          "options": [
            "`\"apple\"`",
            "`42`",
            "`(51.5, -0.1)`",
            "`[\"a\", \"b\"]`"
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Keys must be **hashable**, which in practice means immutable. A list can change, so `{[\"a\", \"b\"]: 1}` raises `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`. Strings, numbers and tuples of immutable values are fine; convert a list to a tuple if you need it as a key."
        },
        {
          "id": "py-quiz-set-ops",
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "```python\na = {1, 2, 3}\nb = {2, 3, 4}\nprint(sorted(a - b), sorted(a & b))\n```\n\nWhat does this print?",
          "options": [
            "`[1] [2, 3]`",
            "`[1, 4] [2, 3]`",
            "`[4] [1, 2, 3, 4]`",
            "`[1] [1, 2, 3, 4]`"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "`a - b` is the **difference**: items in `a` but not in `b`, so `{1}`. `a & b` is the **intersection**: items in both, `{2, 3}`. (`a | b` would be the union, and `a ^ b` the items in exactly one: `{1, 4}`.)"
        }
      ]
